## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of streaming quantiles and the trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and latency. It also evaluates familiarity with mergeable sketches for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantiles (TDigest/KLL)
- Mergeable data sketches across shards
- Compression parameter and accuracy vs memory trade-offs
- Update and query time complexities

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: TDigest usage
class TDigest { 
  add(x); 
  quantile(p); 
  merge(other); 
}

const digest = new TDigest(100);
for (const latency of batch) {
  digest.add(latency);
}
const p95 = digest.quantile(0.95);
```

## Performance Comparison
- **TDigest**: O(n log k) total time, O(k) memory, O(log k) per-element update
- **Recomputing**: O(n log n) total time, O(n) memory, O(1) per-element (but requires storage)

The streaming approach enables real-time 95th percentile computation with strict memory limits and supports distributed merging across shards.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming heavy-hitters knowledge, practical analysis of tradeoffs, and ability to reason about accuracy vs memory.\n\n## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy-hitters algorithm\n- Amortized per-item cost and fixed memory\n- Accuracy guarantees and when top-k is exact vs approximate\n\n## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k; // top-k to track
    this.counters = new Map(); // id -> count
  }
  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k - 1) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      // decrement all counters
      for (let [id, cnt] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
        if (cnt === 1) this.counters.delete(id);
        else this.counters.set(id, cnt - 1);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a sliding window of size W?\n- How do you handle deletions or timestamped streams?\n- What are practical thresholds for choosing k in production?
